<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amritsar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amritsar is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the spiritual centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a>, or Golden Temple. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> it was a major religious, commercial and political centre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> lavished patronage on its shrines. The city remained central to Sikh identity after the empire's annexation by the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amritsar.jpg" alt="Amritsar" width="320" height="131" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shubhashish · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Temple_Amritsar_Gurudwara_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amritsar lies in the Majha region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, 28 kilometres from the India-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> border and 47 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. It takes its name from Amṛta Sarovara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for Pool of Nectar, the pool that surrounds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, and it was formerly also called Rāmdāspur. Tradition links the area to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>: the Bhagwan Valmiki Tirath Sthal is believed to be the ashram of Maharishi Valmiki, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> gave birth to Lava and Kusha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition Guru Ram Das, the fourth guru, founded the city in 1574. One account says that the land was bought with Sikh donations from the owners of the village of Tung, another that Guru Amar Das chose the site. Ram Das first completed the pool, built his centre next to it and invited merchants and artisans from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to settle. The town grew under Guru Arjan Dev, financed by donations and built by volunteers, and in 1604 he installed the scripture of Sikhism in the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a>. Ahmad Shah of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> besieged Amritsar in 1762 and again in 1766 and 1767, massacred its people and destroyed the city.</p><p>Under the Sikh Empire Maharaja Ranjit Singh fortified the city in 1822, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a> later completed the walls with twelve gates. The British annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1849 and built a thirteenth gate in 1866. They supported a textile industry, and Khalsa College made the city a centre of colonial education. On 13 April 1919, the day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaisakhi">Vaisakhi</a>, Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-dyer">Reginald Dyer</a> ordered troops to fire into a crowd of thousands in the Jallianwala Bagh without warning, for about ten minutes. A British government inquiry put the death toll at 379, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> estimated about 1,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and Sialkot districts settled in Amritsar. In June 1984 the Indian army carried out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a> to remove militants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, an action that remains controversial. Official reports gave 83 army deaths and 493 civilians killed, while independent estimates run to more than 5,000. The city is the holiest in Sikhism and the Golden Temple its most-visited gurdwara. In 2011 the municipality had 1,159,227 inhabitants, with Hindus and Sikhs the two main communities, and the city is known for wooden chessboards and chess pieces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amritsar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 13 April 1919 troops under Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer fired on an unarmed crowd at the Jallianwala Bagh in <b>Amritsar</b>, killing hundreds, a massacre that turned many moderates against British rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1762 Ahmad crossed the passes for the sixth time, took <b>Amritsar</b>, massacred thousands of Sikhs in the Vadda Ghallughara and destroyed the Golden Temple; within two years the Sikhs rebelled again and rebuilt their holy city, and his later attempts to subjugate them failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1984 the army's assault on the Golden Temple at <b>Amritsar</b>, Operation Blue Star, was followed by Indira Gandhi's assassination by her Sikh bodyguards and anti-Sikh massacres in Delhi, and in December the gas leak at Bhopal killed thousands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>He was formally proclaimed Maharaja on 12 April 1801, the day of Baisakhi, the spring harvest festival, which gave the proclamation a religious meaning, and in 1802 he took <b>Amritsar</b>, the spiritual centre of Sikhism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The free kitchen (langar) of the Golden Temple at <b>Amritsar</b> serves meals to tens of thousands of people every day, of any faith or none, all of them seated together on the floor as equals.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gandhi called a nationwide strike against it, and on 13 April, in the Jallianwala Bagh massacre at <b>Amritsar</b>, troops under Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer fired without warning on an unarmed crowd, killing 379 people by the official count and many more by Indian estimates.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
